Package: soilcnn
Title: Convolutional Neural Networks for Digital Soil Mapping
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R: c(
    person(c("Cássio", "Marques"), "Moquedace", , "cassiomoquedace@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")),
    person(c("Clara", "Glória", "Oliveira"), "Baldi", role = c("aut", "cph")))
Description: A framework, built on 'torch', for mapping a soil property from
    point observations and a stack of aligned rasters with a convolutional
    network that reads each point's neighbourhood. It extracts the patches;
    resamples by spatial blocks or by k-fold nearest neighbour distance
    matching, after Linnenbrink et al. (2024) <doi:10.5194/gmd-17-5897-2024>;
    tunes and selects under a noise floor and the one-standard-error rule;
    refits the selected configuration under several seeds; and predicts
    median, mean and interval maps over rasters of any extent. The map's
    centre is the ensemble median over seeds; the mean corrects its
    back-transformation by smearing, after Duan (1983)
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1983.10478017>; the intervals are conformal, after
    Lei et al. (2018) <doi:10.1080/01621459.2017.1307116>, calibrated on
    cross-validated residuals, so their coverage is approximate and is checked
    on the test set; and the area of applicability of
    Meyer and Pebesma (2021) <doi:10.1111/2041-210X.13650> is mapped beside
    them. What a model learned is read by permutation, by SHAP values from
    expected gradients, after Erion et al. (2021)
    <doi:10.1038/s42256-021-00343-w>, by SAGE, after Covert et al. (2020)
    <doi:10.48550/arXiv.2004.00668>, by accumulated local effects, after Apley
    and Zhu (2020) <doi:10.1111/rssb.12377>, and by refits without each
    variable. Random forest and multilayer perceptron baselines run under the
    same folds.
